<h1>My Objects</h1>
<p>take one of your 10 Books in Excel books and create a BoxLang structure out of it's metadata. 
    Use multiple structures to model as much of the metadata as you can including data from all four Excel Sheets 
    (or SQL Tables). When you are done, dump out each of the objects you created.</p>





<h3>My Objects</h3>

<p>
    Take one of your 10 Books in Excel books and create
    a BoxLang structure out of its metadata.
    Use multiple structures to model as much of the
    metadata as you can, including data from your
    Excel sheets. When you are done, dump out
    each of the objects you created.
</p>

<bx:set myBook = {
    title: "The Paris Architect",
    pages: 384,
    author: {
        firstName: "Charles",
        lastName: "Belfoure"
    },
    publisher: {
        name: "Sourcebooks Landmark",
        location: "United States"
    },
    yearPublished: 2013,
    genres: ["Historical Fiction"],
    format: "Paperback",
    language: "English",
    condition: "Good",
    publicationCountry: "United States"
} />

<bx:output>

    <p>
        Dot Notation:
        #myBook.title# by
        #myBook.author.firstName#
        #myBook.author.lastName#
    </p>

    <p>
        Bracket Notation:
        #myBook["title"]# by
        #myBook["author"]["firstName"]#
        #myBook["author"]["lastName"]#
    </p>

    <p>
        Genre: #myBook.genres[1]#
    </p>

</bx:output>

<bx:dump var="#myBook#" />
